Open the asymmetric assembly path to user elements, and stop truncating the
node count at 127.

U3 (F-barES-FEM-T4 volumetric, patch 0002) has a non-symmetric element
matrix by construction.  Eq. (17) of Onishi, Iida & Amaya, verbatim:

  "Note that the stretching tensor in this equation, D~, is not the
   deformation rate of F in Eq. (11) but that of F~ in Eq. (1) due to the
   adoption of the F-bar method."

The stress comes from the modified gradient, the virtual work is paired with
the unmodified one, and the result is K_vol = (K V_h) tbar^T sbar with
tbar != sbar.  Assembling the Galerkin form sbar^T sbar instead measures
within 0.02% on C1111 but is a different element.

ccx already has a complete asymmetric path -- elements raise nasym,
mafillsmas assembles, pardiso.c selects mtype=11 -- built for friction
contact.  Three things stopped U3 from reaching it.

1. nasym WAS RAISED TOO LATE.

Contact sets nasym in gencontelem_*, before the assembly.  An element that
raises it inside e_c3d_u is already too late: mafillsm walks only the upper
triangle of the element matrix (do ll=jj,...) into symmetric storage, so a
U3 that reached it would be silently symmetrised, and mafillsmas would then
add the full matrix on top of that -- wrong twice over.

  linstatic.c    scan lakon for "U3" and set nasym before mafillsmmain
  nonlingeo.c    the same, before its own mafillsmmain
  mafillsm.f     skip U3 when nasym is set, exactly as it already skips
                 friction springs ('ES...C')

2. THE ASYMMETRIC MECHANICAL LOOP COVERED ONLY CONTACT ELEMENTS.

Friction contact is stock ccx's only asymmetric mechanical contribution, and
every contact element is generated past ne0, so mafillsmasmain partitions
the mechanical range over ne0+1..ne -- and with no contact it sets
nea = ne0+1 > neb = ne0, an EMPTY range.

U3 lives among the ordinary elements.  With the stock bounds mafillsmas
walked nothing, so the entire volumetric half of the element vanished from
the matrix while neq and the sparsity stayed correct (mastruct builds those
from the connectivity, not from the assembly).  The patch test came back
with interior displacements 200x the applied field: a model held together by
its deviatoric term alone.

  mafillsmasmain.c  when the model contains a U3, partition the mechanical
                    range over 1..ne, as the thermal range already is.  The
                    loop cycles on anything that is neither 'ES' nor 'U3',
                    so widening it is safe; models without U3 keep the
                    stock contact partition and its load balance.
  mafillsmas.f      a U3 branch calling e_c3d_u3 into wide su/smu/ffu, and
                    sval selecting su or s in the one shared assembly block
                    (the same arrangement patch 0006 used in mafillsm).
                    su/smu are ALLOCATABLE and are only sized to 765 when
                    the thread's range actually contains a U3: 765*765*8 =
                    4.7 MB each as a fixed local would land in .bss and be
                    shared across the assembly threads (patch 0007).
                    Verified: .bss is still 0 in mafillsmas.o.

3. THE NODE COUNT WAS READ AS A SIGNED CHAR.

  mastruct.c, mastructcs.c, arpackcs.c:   nope = lakon[8*i+7];

lakon is char*, char is SIGNED on x86-64, and the node count is one byte.
A user element of more than 127 nodes was therefore read here as a NEGATIVE
nope and got no matrix structure at all.  The Fortran side is unaffected
(ichar returns 0..255), so the assembly went on to ask for entries mastruct
had never created and add_sm_st_as stopped with 'coefficient should be 0'.

Patch 0001 raised the element matrix to 765 DOF on the argument that 255
nodes is what lakon(8:8) can encode and userelements.f allows.  That was
right about the Fortran and wrong about the C: the real ceiling was 127.
An F-barES-FEM-T4 c=2 stencil of 138 nodes hit it, on the first deck that
used one.  Fixed with an (unsigned char) cast in all five places.

VERIFICATION

  * patch test and confined compression, c = 0, 1 and 2, agree with the
    C3D4 control to the last printed digit (patch 0002 has the table).
    c = 2 exercises a 414-DOF element and is the case that found defect 3.
  * ccx reports 'Factoring the system of equations using the unsymmetric
    pardiso solver' for every U3 deck, c = 0 included.
  * global force balance |sum RF| / max|RF| <= 7.1e-15, so the internal
    forces recovered by resultsmech_u3 match the matrix that mafillsmas
    assembled -- the two enter through completely separate paths.

